WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.356
A modernization program spends
most of its attention on day one:

2
00:00:04.356 --> 00:00:08.500
the tenant is built, identities
synchronize, the cutover runs.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.059
The estate is judged on
day two, when every reset,

4
00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:15.162
unlock and new hire
lands at the help desk.

5
00:00:15.162 --> 00:00:18.537
In Entra ID the help
desk's power is explicit.

6
00:00:18.537 --> 00:00:23.294
Microsoft labels the Helpdesk Administrator
role privileged, and warns that

7
00:00:23.294 --> 00:00:27.976
changing a user's password may mean the
ability to assume that user's identity.

8
00:00:27.976 --> 00:00:31.643
Requiring M F A re-registration
deletes the user's phones,

9
00:00:31.643 --> 00:00:34.999
Authenticator registrations
and software tokens,

10
00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:39.525
and whoever signs in next and registers
a method controls the account.

11
00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:43.750
So a reset on an unverified
request isn't a support task.

12
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.562
It's a transfer of the
account to whoever asked.

13
00:00:46.562 --> 00:00:47.762
Attackers know it.

14
00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:52.373
CISA's advisory on the Scattered
Spider group describes calls that

15
00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:57.063
convinced help desk staff to reset
passwords and transfer M F A tokens.

16
00:00:57.063 --> 00:01:01.022
And when the governed path is
hard, the desk works around it:

17
00:01:01.022 --> 00:01:04.899
an emergency admin account, an
email treated as a request,

18
00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:08.260
a temporary password sent
back to the same inbox.
